{"kind":"assert","text":"AR-7 trace and proposal (request 02989375, promise 45576f7c). Read-only, at main a7acfdf2.\n\nCause: the indexedScopes adapter (packages/scope/src/scope-index.ts:150) awaits ScopeIndex.has(name) before every method call on a DeployedScope target. nativeBirthByName adds a second has (:146). The answers are not cached, on purpose: the comment at :66-68 reads \"Cache transport identity only, not has()/registration/proof answers\". The adapter wraps both Worker ingress (apps/room/fetch.ts:19 → worker.ts:513 route) and every scope-to-scope RPC (DeployedScope[scopeNamespace], worker.ts:808 → namespace.ts deliver/source/nativeBirth/observe). Alarms bypass it. So ScopeIndex requests ≈ scope requests − alarms. Measured: 25 scope requests a minute (13+4+4+4) − 1 Membership alarm = 24. That matches.\n\nPer tick, estimated (part read, part inferred):\n- Simulator HTTP: 2 Membership session POSTs (simulator-host.ts:80-81; the climate session is opened and discarded) plus about 9 Room A reads and writes (simulator-tick.ts:120-181: summary ×3, entries ×3, history, observe, report-site) = about 11. Read.\n- report-site relate to Site: Room A → Site.deliver 1; Site's receiverReads, its own birth by RPC 1 and the Directory birth 1 (partition-proof.ts:231, :252; this matches the :10.0/:10.7 log order); Site reads Room A's source 1-2; the result back to Room A 1; Room A's own birth 1-2; Room A reads Site's source 1; Membership observe 0-1 (300 s window). About 8-11. Partly inferred.\n- Total about 19-25 a tick.\n\nProposal, smallest first:\nP1 (simulator, examples/grow only): drop the unused climate session (simulator-host.ts:81) and reuse the tick's summary read where the tick does not need a fresh one (simulator-tick.ts:120/144/173). Saves about 3 ScopeIndex and about 3 scope requests a minute: 49 → about 43. It touches no platform security seal. It does need a new simulator deployment, and if the simulator release is pinned, a version step under the application version rule.\nP2 (platform, scope-index.ts:149-151): cache positive has(name[, kind]) answers in isolate memory, keyed by binding, mode and name; never cache a negative or an error; bound it at 4096 entries. Rows are insert-only (register uses ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING and stores genesis and kind once; scope-index.ts:31-36), so a positive answer cannot go stale. A retired room still answers its retired stop from the object (object.ts:539). Expected: ScopeIndex 24 → about 2-5 a minute (warm isolates hit; cold Worker and DO isolates miss). That brings grow to about 27-30 a minute, roughly 1,200 fewer requests an hour. Costs: it reverses the design note at :66-68; it moves the seal at security-entry-points.test.mjs:292; it needs a threat-model line (cache key, bound, positive-only).\nP3 (namespace.ts:443-453): read one's own birth locally, not by RPC through the adapter. Saves about 2 ScopeIndex and 2 scope requests a tick. It touches a sealed file for a smaller gain than P2.\nRejected: passing resolved ids along the chain to bypass the adapter. That removes the gate that stops arbitrary names from activating empty objects (scope-index.ts:72-75).\n\nI recommend P2: it is one function, it is behind the existing gate, and it removes most of the cost. P1 is a cheap demo-only follow-up. The security note in :66-68 was a deliberate choice, so P2 is yours (or Hugh's) to rule on. Which do you want?","body":{"to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}